{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 12, :comments ["<b>One who strikes a man and he dies shall surely be put to death.</b> Many have endeavored to establish an order for all the commandments in this parashah, and so I found in Toldot Yitzhak and in Mahar'i, but their words do not seem right in my eyes, therefore I did not see fit to elaborate on their mention. And I say: just as He began with ״כִּי תִקְנֶה עֶבֶד עִבְרִי״ (Exodus 21:2), for it corresponds to the utterance of ״אָנֹכִי״, and the utterance of ״אָנֹכִי״ surely encompasses all the commandments between man and HaShem up to ״לֹא תִּרְצָח״, so too He began here with ״מַכֵּה אִישׁ״, which is the utterance of ״לֹא תִּרְצָח״, and it corresponds to ״אָנֹכִי״ and is the head of the five latter commandments that are between man and his fellow, and ״לֹא תִּרְצָח״ encompasses all of them. For even one who commits injustice with money is called ״מַכֵּה אִישׁ״, for he wounds a living soul. And through the goodness of pure providence, all ten commandments are remembered in this parashah. And were I to add reasons for every commandment, the account would grow long, and I have already agreed to shorten this composition as much as possible, therefore I have withdrawn my hand from this labor, for it is too great for me. Yet from among all of them I decided to add reasons regarding the laws of the thief, for there are in it several distinct laws: for one who steals a man and sells him is as though he handed him over to death, for the buyer has in his hand to do with him whatever he wishes, for all the conditions of captivity apply to him (Bava Batra 8a); therefore his law is death. And one who steals money pays double, for a man does not depart from the world with half his desire in his hand — he has in his hand a hundred yet covets two hundred, and so forth (Ecclesiastes Rabbah 1:13). So too this thief, who did not lack what was in his hand — surely all day long he covets the desire of the double; therefore his law is to pay double. And a hint to the matter: מָמוֹן is from the doubled letters, when you write מ״ם ו״ו נוּ״ן. But ordinary robbers break in and do not covet the double. And furthermore, the thief has stolen two thefts: the money of its owner and the knowledge of its buyer, which is not so with the robber." "And regarding the fourfold and fivefold payment for stealing an ox or a sheep and slaughtering or selling it: this is because its blood is sought — so it is expounded, and also to include the value of its seed, for the male ox and the male sheep were fit to sire offspring. And we find in the Torah that Jacob sent forty cows and ten bulls, for every bull is fit to sire four cows; therefore he gives four female cattle in place of the ox, and a fifth animal as a penalty on account of the double. And according to this calculation he should have had to give eleven female sheep in place of the male sheep, for every ram requires ten females, and one more on account of the penalty — except that the Torah had pity on him on account of the humiliation that the sheep bore upon his shoulder, and it deducted seven from him on account of the humiliation, for great is the dignity of creatures — it even pushes aside seven sheep. And there is support for this from the verse (Proverbs 6:30–31): ״אַל יָבוּזוּ לַגַּנָּב כִּי יִגְנוֹב״ and so forth, ״וְנִמְצָא יְשַׁלֵּם שִׁבְעָתָיִם״. TheRalbag explained that if the thief is found in the house of the owner of the stolen goods, when he gives him seven for one, and so forth — it turns out that this one gives seven on account of his humiliation, for behold, from the reasoning of ״יְשַׁלֵּם שִׁבְעָתָיִם״ he said ״אַל יָבוּזוּ לַגַּנָּב״. And from where did Solomon learn to say thus, that he gives seven on account of his humiliation? Only from the deduction of seven sheep from eleven, as stated."], :book :exodus, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 21, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:22:25.530438167Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}